Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Memory-context chunks as Sublet lifetimes (CDERIVE/CREVOKE)
Upstream: postgresql 17.0
Upstream-Archive: https://ftp.postgresql.org/pub/source/v17.0/postgresql-17.0.tar.bz2
Upstream-SHA256: 7e276131c0fdd6b62588dbad9b3bb24b8c3498d5009328dba59af16e819109de
Build-Variants: Sublet replay; the 17.5 server with PGSU_NESTED=sublet
Depends-On: postgresql-17.0-0001-allocset-capstone-size-classes.patch
  postgresql-17.0-0002-memorychunk-capstone-alignment.patch
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

Every chunk a memory context hands out is a child lifetime of its block,
bounded to the request; pfree and repalloc revoke it, so every copy of the
caller's pointer dies with it. The 17.0 and 17.5 manager sources are
identical, so the same patch serves the replay and the server.

# Two instructions do the work. CDERIVE makes a child capability of a
# non-linear parent, bounded to a sub-range and carrying MANAGE over its own
# children; CREVOKE(parent, child) ends a direct child and everything below
# it, and faults when the child is not a live direct child.
#
# The protection sits at the dispatch, not inside the context types. mcxt.c
# issues a bounded child on the way out of every allocation entry and
# revokes it on the way back in (pfree, repalloc), then hands the context
# method the allocator's own pointer at the same address. The four context
# types keep working on pointers that span their whole malloc'd object,
# exactly as upstream: headers, free lists and block arithmetic unchanged.
#
# pfree has only the pointer, and a bounded pointer cannot reach the chunk
# header in front of it. So sublet.c keeps the blocks in a table sorted by
# address. Each block has a lifetime derived from its malloc'd object, and a
# generation below that from which the chunks are derived. The context types
# only report their blocks:
#
#   SubletBlockAdd     a new block (keeper at create, new and large blocks)
#   SubletBlockForget  before the block's free(); freeing the malloc'd
#                      object revokes the system allocator's lifetime of it,
#                      and every chunk lies below that
#   SubletBlockRenew   a reset that keeps the block: the generation is
#                      revoked and a new one derived
#
# A recycled AllocSet passes through MemoryContextResetOnly first, so its
# keeper is renewed there. alignedalloc.c needs no change: its redirect
# chunks go through the same dispatch. Bump has no pfree upstream, and
# keeps none.
